Z||000008||Shaun41178||01-05-2000||01:44 AM||||Since there isn't a kill section I have just put all my racing stories here in the General chat section of the Forum.<P>You can probably do a search of "Fiero vs".  That is how I usually title my race stories here depending on which car I race.  Or you can just do a search of my posts alone.  Many of them will be about kill stories.<P>If there ever becomes a kill section I will put them in there but untill then.  Besides if we ever did get a kill section there probably would only be a few posts and those would most likely be few and far between cause as far as i can tell most people on this forum don't race their Fieros.<P>I do have a suggestion however for a new section.  Perhaps a section dedicated to Engine upgrades to the V6 and the 4.  Perhaps a section for each.  This way all the topics on engine upgrades and 3.1's and such can be kept all in the same area and make it easier to find.  Cliff any feelings about that?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||01-05-2000||05:43 AM||||Racing is what Fieros do best.  But smoking tires from stoplight to stoplihgt is a poor substitute for a race.  The road from Fairbanks to Nenana goes uphill for about five miles then zigzags back and forth across the ridgeline beside the Tanana river for another forty miles. At the right times there is less than one car every twenty miles.  I guess camaros get raced so often is there are so many of them.  Ran up behind a TR7 just past the uphill and he did not want to be passed.  Being a cool dry day and no traffic I determined my 88GT would pass.  More power and familiarity with the road put me past him in about six miles, but then he would not get off my tail.  I guess he figured if I went off the road he would slow down.  I finally (repitition,repitition,repitition) had all four tires making noise on every single corner (75 to 90 MPH) and he disappeared finally because the Fiero just plain outperformed the TR7 when they were both pushed to the limit.  50 miles in 27 minutes and all done on my own side of the road.  I didn't get to meet the other driver but he drove his car as hard as it would go and also stayed on his side of the road, difficult to find folks like that driving new cars. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||01-05-2000||03:49 PM||||I just got killed today.  By, guess what, a Dodge Shadow with 0 mods.  I've got about 1,000 miles on the 3.1 and just changed the oil yesterday.  All ready to go and I just didn't have enough to beat this guy.  I'd rather be assured that I'm a bad driver and the other guy's really good than my car has something wrong with it.  Honestly, it's a lot faster than it used to be.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroMike||01-05-2000||04:17 PM||||Eric,<BR>I think it has the same or close to engine, with better breathing... ie DOHC, or SOHC.  So it probably pumps a little more.  I don't know though.  I had a rebuilt 2.8 and had no problem beating any v-6 un-modded.  Especially a DODGE!  Do you have an AUTO or V-6?||||reg||1
Z||000012||2birds||01-06-2000||11:10 PM||||Don't forget, Mopar is famous for sleepers.  Some Shadows came with turbo motors, and they'll run real hard, even stock.  Ever seen an Omni GLH?  The GLH stands for Goes Like Hell...  Only real cue was the aluminum wheels.  You have to respect what Mopar and Mitsubishi came up with.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||01-07-2000||05:30 AM||||How about the Omni GLHS?  That was even quicker.  But I still love my Fiero and will defend her to the death!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||01-07-2000||01:38 PM||||Monkeyman, those little Mopars were the rage back when I joined the Navy in '85.  I test drove a GLHS at a Dodge dealer in Orlando, but as an 18 year old Sailor, the insurance would have killed me.  Had to wait until I made enough money to afford one, and then the  Fiero showed up.  ||||reg||1
